{
  "info": {
    "name": "Relay \u00b7 scaling and load balancing",
    "_postman_id": "b7e4b6a1-5c3f-4a92-9a1e-relay-scaling",
    "description": "Load-balancing checks for Relay, over HTTP.\n\nThe question each folder asks:\n  1. Is the proxy actually spreading traffic across every replica?\n  2. Given that it is, does every replica give the SAME answer about shared state?\n\nBoth matter, and the second is meaningless without the first \u2014 a collection that quietly ran against one replica would pass every agreement check while proving nothing. So the distribution test asserts it saw `expectedReplicas` distinct instances, and fails if it did not.\n\nAttribution comes from the `X-Relay-Instance` response header.\n\nWHAT THIS DELIBERATELY DOES NOT COVER: the realtime side. The core of multi-instance is that a message published on one replica reaches WebSocket clients on the others, and Postman's WebSocket requests cannot run inside a collection or carry test scripts. `scripts/probe-scaling.mjs` and `tests/realtime.test.mjs` cover that half; this covers the HTTP half. Do not read a green run here as 'multi-instance works'.\n\nRun it in the Collection Runner or with Newman \u2014 several requests loop themselves via `postman.setNextRequest`, which a single Send ignores:\n\n  docker compose up -d --scale api=3\n  npm run test:postman\n\nSet `expectedReplicas` to match your scale.",
    "schema": "https://schema.getpostman.com/json/collection/v2.1.0/collection.json"
  },
